---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import EmberClick from '../components/EmberClick.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={`About | ${SITE_TITLE}`} description="The story behind the work. Meredith McGee writes about how she got from Rochester to Yale to Boston, and why public health keeps pulling her in." />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />

		<!-- Page Heading -->
		<section class="about-heading bg-dark bg-pattern">
			<div class="container animate-on-scroll">
				<h1>Hi, I'm Meredith</h1>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- Essay Body -->
		<section class="about-essay bg-light">
			<div class="essay-body">
				<p>
					I grew up in Rochester, New York, where I first figured out that I learn best by going all in. Most of what I loved as a kid looked unrelated on the surface: stage managing theater productions, training for track and field, spending my free time on whatever corner of history I couldn't stop thinking about. But each one was teaching me to see how things connect, how every part of a system shapes the others, whether that's a scene change that shifts the orchestra's timing and the entire feel of an act, how foot positioning in the ring can change the trajectory of an entire throw, or how a single policy decision ripples through a community for generations. I've never been able to pick just one thing, which is how I ended up at Lafayette studying both biology and history.
				</p>

				<p>
					Earning a B.S. in biology and a B.A. in history meant I got the "why don't you just pick one?" question a lot. But to me, both were asking the same question in different languages: how do complex systems work, and what happens to the people inside them? I competed in Division I track and field, <span class="footnote-trigger" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-describedby="footnote-tooltip" data-aside="The hammer is a 16-pound metal ball on a steel wire. You spin in a seven-foot circle and try to throw it as far as possible. It takes years to convince your body to trust the chaos of it, and I loved it completely.">throwing the hammer</span> until my body and health had other plans. But stepping away from athletics pushed me toward the thing I actually wanted to spend my energy on: building something for my community, and I wouldn't trade how that turned out.
				</p>

				<figure class="essay-photo">
					<img src="/meredith-headshot.jpg" alt="Meredith McGee" />
				</figure>

				<p>
					Lafayette didn't have enough support for LGBTQ+ students when I arrived, so I helped change that. My partner K and I co-founded OUT Lafayette to create space for LGBTQ+ students to gather, and we launched <span class="footnote-trigger" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-describedby="footnote-tooltip" data-aside="We converted a former sorority house into gender-neutral housing, which sounds simple until you realize how many institutional hurdles a sorority house has. It became a real home. I still think about the students who needed it before it existed.">Lavender Lane</span>, a living-learning community in a converted sorority house that became the college's first gender-affirming and gender-neutral housing option. This culminated in the project I'm still proudest of: bringing panels of the National AIDS Memorial Quilt to campus for the first time in three decades.
				</p>

				<p>
					I also got <span class="ember-trigger" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Click to meet Ember">Ember</span> during college, a black lab who has been my best friend ever since.
				</p>

				<p>
					After Lafayette, I went to the Yale School of Public Health for my MPH in Social and Behavioral Sciences. Yale gave me the formal training to match instincts I'd been acting on for years. I worked as a graduate research assistant at the LGBTQ+ Mental Health Initiative, where I contributed to an affirmative cognitive behavioral therapy trial and used multi-state BRFSS data to analyze how cannabis use patterns differ by sexual identity. I also designed and led an evaluation of how students experienced their public health internships, turning what I heard into recommendations that changed how the program prepared students for the field.
				</p>

				<p>
					Yale was also where I started doing policy work. I completed an analysis for the U.S. Senate Finance Committee examining outcomes for youth in congregate care settings, and dug into how Housing First policies actually play out for the people they're designed to reach. Those projects taught me something I keep coming back to: the distance between how a system is supposed to work on paper and how people actually experience it is where some of the most important questions live.
				</p>

				<p>
					While I was at Yale, I was also working as a grant specialist at Bradbury-Sullivan LGBT Community Center in Pennsylvania. That role taught me that development is, in itself, public health work. Every proposal I write is built around community needs, program evidence, and data, structured into a narrative that makes a funder understand why this work matters and what it changes. I've helped secure <span class="footnote-trigger" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-describedby="footnote-tooltip" data-aside="That number is made up of individual awards, most of them hard-won and none of them guaranteed. Each one funded something real: a staff position, a health program, a year of services for people who needed them. It doesn't feel like a big number when you see what it's actually doing.">over a million dollars</span> in funding for health equity and community programs, and I think of every one of those proposals as a case for why a community deserves resources it shouldn't have to ask for.
				</p>

				<p>
					These days, I'm in Boston. I'm a Junior Public Health Researcher at Parabola Center for Law and Policy, where I study how cannabis legalization actually shapes public health outcomes. The center's framework is built around the drug policy parabola: the idea that both prohibition and unregulated commercial markets cause harm, and that the best policy lies somewhere between them. My research focuses on what that middle ground looks like in practice: how people experience these markets, where policy design falls short, and what it would take for legalization to deliver on its promise.
				</p>

				<p>
					I also publish Ground Level, a Substack about cannabis, public health, and the space between policy and practice. And I still write grants at Bradbury-Sullivan, because that work matters and I'm good at it.
				</p>

				<figure class="essay-photo">
					<img src="/family-photo.jpg" alt="Meredith with K and Ember" />
				</figure>

				<p>
					If there's a thread through all of this, it's curiosity about systems and a stubborn belief that the people inside those systems deserve better information, better programs, and better policy than they usually get. I'm a researcher and a writer, but what I really am is someone who can't stop asking whether things are working the way they should, and who uses writing to figure out the answer and share it with the people who need it.
				</p>

				<p>
					I live with my partner, K, and <span class="ember-trigger" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Click to meet Ember">Ember</span> (the aforementioned black lab) in Boston. Outside of work, I'm trying to learn how to cook, I read across disciplines because I can't help it, and I spend as much time outside and with dogs as I can.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div id="footnote-tooltip" class="footnote-tooltip" role="tooltip" aria-hidden="true">
				<p class="footnote-text"></p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<EmberClick />
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	/* Page Heading Section */
	.about-heading {
		padding: 5rem 0 3.5rem;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.about-heading .container {
		max-width: 800px;
		padding: 0 2rem;
	}

	.about-heading h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.75rem);
		margin: 0;
		line-height: 1.1;
	}

	/* Essay Section */
	.about-essay {
		padding: 4.5rem 0 5rem;
	}

	/* Essay Body Container */
	.essay-body {
		max-width: 800px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 0 2rem;
	}

	.essay-body p {
		margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
		font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1.1vw, 1.05rem);
		line-height: 1.8;
	}

	.essay-body p:last-child {
		margin-bottom: 0;
	}

	/* Inline Essay Photos */
	.essay-photo {
		max-width: 350px;
		margin: 2.5rem auto;
		border-radius: 12px;
		overflow: hidden;
		box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);
	}

	.essay-photo img {
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		display: block;
	}

	/* Footnote Triggers */
	.footnote-trigger {
		border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--color-text-muted);
		cursor: pointer;
		touch-action: manipulation;
		transition: border-bottom-color 0.2s ease;
	}

	.footnote-trigger:hover {
		border-bottom-color: var(--color-gold);
	}

	/* Footnote Tooltip */
	.footnote-tooltip {
		position: fixed;
		max-width: 280px;
		padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
		background: var(--color-dark);
		border: 1px solid var(--color-gold);
		border-radius: 8px;
		box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
		z-index: 1001;
		opacity: 0;
		pointer-events: none;
		transition: opacity 0.2s ease;
	}

	.footnote-tooltip.visible {
		opacity: 1;
		pointer-events: auto;
	}

	.footnote-tooltip .footnote-text {
		color: var(--color-cream);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		line-height: 1.5;
		font-style: italic;
		margin: 0;
	}

	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.footnote-trigger {
			transition: none;
		}

		.footnote-tooltip {
			transition: none;
		}
	}

	/* Responsive */
	@media (max-width: 768px) {
		.about-heading {
			padding: 4rem 0 2.5rem;
		}

		.essay-body {
			padding: 0 1.5rem;
		}

		.essay-photo {
			max-width: 280px;
		}
	}
</style>

<script>
	document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
		const tooltip = document.getElementById('footnote-tooltip');
		const tooltipText = tooltip?.querySelector('.footnote-text');
		if (!tooltip || !tooltipText) return;

		const triggers = document.querySelectorAll('.footnote-trigger');
		if (triggers.length === 0) return;

		triggers.forEach(trigger => {
			trigger.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
				e.stopPropagation();
				const aside = trigger.getAttribute('data-aside');
				if (!aside) return;

				tooltipText.textContent = aside;

				// Position near click with viewport clamping
				const TOOLTIP_WIDTH = 280;
				const x = Math.min(e.clientX + 10, window.innerWidth - TOOLTIP_WIDTH - 15);
				const y = Math.max(e.clientY - 80, 80);
				tooltip.style.left = `${x}px`;
				tooltip.style.top = `${y}px`;

				tooltip.classList.add('visible');
				tooltip.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'false');
			});

			// Keyboard support
			trigger.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
				if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') {
					e.preventDefault();
					trigger.click();
				}
			});
		});

		// Dismiss on click anywhere else
		document.addEventListener('click', () => {
			tooltip.classList.remove('visible');
			tooltip.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
		});

		// Dismiss on Escape key
		document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
			if (e.key === 'Escape') {
				tooltip.classList.remove('visible');
				tooltip.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
			}
		});
	});
</script>
